---
// ChatJS's story, from Francisco Moretti's answers (2026-10-08); the page
// counts, dates, and pull requests are checked against the public
// FranciscoMoretti/chat-js repository and chatjs.dev/docs's sitemap.
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import Quote from "../_customers/Quote.astro";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("chatjs")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://chatjs.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>ChatJS</a> is an
    open-source foundation for building AI apps. It starts developers with a
    working chat application, with authentication, saved conversations, and
    streaming responses, then lets them choose their AI providers and add
    capabilities like web search, image generation, code execution, and MCP
    integrations as they need them. Installed tools live as editable source in
    the project, so every app can make the interface and behavior its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    ChatJS is built with Next.js, React, TypeScript, and the AI SDK, and its
    docs cover all of it: guides, a page for every feature, the gateways and
    tools, the CLI, and a cookbook of patterns to copy into any project.
  </p>

  <h2>Looking for the balance</h2>
  <p>
    ChatJS's docs started on Mintlify. With the plan up for renewal in a couple
    of months, Francisco Moretti, ChatJS's creator, reconsidered the setup. He
    had found himself shaping the docs to fit the platform, and he wanted more
    room to customize them.
  </p>
  <p>
    He had been at the other end before, too. He'd built a docs site of his own
    with Next.js, and while the control was great, getting it to feel polished
    was a project in itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blume sat between the two: a docs site that worked from the start, with the
    freedom to customize or extend it later. ChatJS's docs were already
    Markdown, written and changed through code, so Blume fit the way Francisco
    already worked.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/francisco-moretti.webp"
    name="Francisco Moretti"
    role="Creator, ChatJS"
  >
    Blume was exactly what I needed: something I could start with that just
    works, knowing I can customize or extend it later.
  </Quote>

  <h2>The move</h2>
  <p>
    Francisco moved the docs on July 14, 2026, the day after Blume 1.0 came
    out. He handled it in a single AI chat: he pointed the agent at Blume,
    asked it to migrate the docs, and barely had to steer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The content was already in a portable format, so most of the work was
    around it. 71 pages moved, from guides and feature docs to reference pages
    and cookbook recipes. The navigation moved from Mintlify's{" "}
    <code>docs.json</code> into <code>blume.config.ts</code>, the callouts and
    accordions were adapted to Blume's, and the icons and images came along.
  </p>
  <p>
    No page changed its path, so every URL under chatjs.dev/docs kept working.
    A{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#subpath-deploys"><code>base</code></a> of{" "}
    <code>/docs</code> builds the docs as a static site under that path. They
    deploy to Vercel as their own project from the ChatJS monorepo, and
    chatjs.dev routes <code>/docs</code> to them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before the switch,{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cli/validate"><code>blume validate</code></a> checked every
    link, and browser checks covered the rendered pages. The{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/FranciscoMoretti/chat-js/pull/230"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>whole move</a
    > was opened and merged the same day.
  </p>
  <p>
    Checking the migrated pages also turned up outdated examples and links that
    predated Blume, and Francisco used the move to clean them up. Since then,
    CI checks every internal and external link whenever the docs change, and a
    broken one fails the check.
  </p>

  <h2>Fixing it in Blume</h2>
  <p>
    One small thing didn't match. In dark mode, the sidebar's native scrollbar
    stayed light: Blume switched its own colors with the theme but never told
    the browser, so native controls kept their default light look. Another
    Blume user had reported it that morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Francisco investigated and fixed it in Blume itself. That evening he opened
    a{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/haydenbleasel/blume/pull/59"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>pull request</a
    > that sets the browser's <code>color-scheme</code> to match the active theme,
    with tests and a changeset. It was merged within hours and released in Blume
    1.0.4 the next morning, and ChatJS{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/FranciscoMoretti/chat-js/pull/232"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>upgraded</a
    > an hour and a half later: under 12 hours from opening the fix to running it
    on chatjs.dev.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was the flexibility Francisco had been looking for. He didn't need to
    build a docs platform himself, but when he needed to improve this one, he
    could.
  </p>

  <h2>Room to grow</h2>
  <p>
    The docs have kept growing with ChatJS. They're at 82 pages now, up from
    71, with new sections for tools, file storage, and the registry, and six{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#redirects">redirects</a> keep the addresses of
    pages that moved along the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    In September, Francisco upgraded to Blume 1.6.3 and picked up features that
    had shipped since: an <a href="/docs/cli/evals">eval suite</a> of questions
    ChatJS's users ask, like how to deploy to Vercel, which{" "}
    <code>blume eval</code> answers from the docs alone, and the monorepo's
    agent skills, which the docs now{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/agent-discovery#skills-discovery">publish</a>{" "}
    for coding agents to find.
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    The move took a single AI chat and kept every URL, and the docs have run
    without deployment work since.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">What</th>
        <th scope="col">Number</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Pages moved</th><td>71, from guides to cookbook recipes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Pages today</th><td>82</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">URLs changed in the move</th><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Blume fix, opened to deployed</th><td>Under 12 hours</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Docs deployment work since</th><td>None</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    For Francisco, the value is a polished starting point that fits how he
    works, with the freedom to change it later. And the time it frees goes back
    into ChatJS.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/francisco-moretti.webp"
    name="Francisco Moretti"
    role="Creator, ChatJS"
  >
    Since moving to Blume, I haven't had to spend time on docs deployment.
  </Quote>

  <p>
    The docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://chatjs.dev/docs" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>chatjs.dev/docs</a>,
    and their source is{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/FranciscoMoretti/chat-js/tree/main/apps/docs"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>public on GitHub</a
    >. To make the same move, follow the{" "}
    <a href="/guides/migrate-from-mintlify">Mintlify migration guide</a>.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
